{
  "id": "linked-list-cycle",
  "questionId": "linked-list-cycle",
  "slug": "linked-list-cycle",
  "title": "Linked List Cycle",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "cycle detection",
    "linked list"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "grind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you could track every index you've visited in a set, but can you detect revisiting a node without extra memory?",
    "imagine two people walking the same track, one twice as fast as the other - if the track loops, what eventually happens to the gap between them?",
    "if there's no cycle, the fast pointer (moving 2 steps at a time) will reach the end of the list and stop",
    "if there is a cycle, the fast pointer will eventually catch up to and land on the exact same node as the slow pointer - that's your signal"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "not handling pos = -1 as a normal list ending, and instead treating index -1 as a valid wraparound target",
    "forgetting to check whether fast (or fast's next) is at the end before advancing it two steps",
    "comparing values instead of node positions/identity when checking if fast and slow have met"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is described by an array `nums`, where `nums[i]`\nis the value of the i-th node and node i normally points to node i+1.\nYou're also given an integer `pos`: the 0-indexed position that the\nlast node's next pointer connects back to, instead of ending the list\nnormally. Reason about the list as if you were traversing it node by\nnode with a next pointer, wrapping around to index `pos` after the\nlast element when `pos != -1`.\n\n**Determine whether the list has a cycle.**\n\nIf `pos` is -1, the list has no cycle and simply ends after the last\nnode.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^4",
      "-10^5 <= nums[i] <= 10^5",
      "-1 <= pos < nums.length"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "pos = -1 (no cycle)",
      "a single-node list that cycles to itself (pos = 0)",
      "the cycle starts at the head (pos = 0) versus somewhere in the middle"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [3, 2, 0, -4], pos = 1",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "the last node (-4) points back to index 1 (value 2), forming a cycle"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2], pos = 0",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "the last node (2) points back to index 0 (value 1)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1], pos = -1",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "single node, no cycle, the list just ends"
      }
    ],
